> Only if you're interested.

Yeah, I was curious enough to adjust the benchmarks at least. Turns out you were right to push back on the cache misses, because indeed with just this PR, cold-access to properties slows down by about 6.5% (measured both cycles and wall time). This is mainly due to the PR in its current form reading from three memory locations:

1. the metadata slot (`AHashMap`)
2. the map entry (`AHashMap`)
3. the separately stored `Member` (`PagedAllocator`)

Whereas on current `master`, we're done at the second step. With my follow-up it would reduce again to two steps:

1. the slot (`Vector`)
2. the Member (`PagedAllocator`)

And it would be about 1-2% faster than current master, even measured by cycles/time on cold cache (10% less instructions appears to compensate well for the more frequent cache misses). I have updated [the benchmarks](https://gist.github.com/bjorn/a8f9aff7e40ca35ed6f9101459f83131#example-results) to include evicting the cache by reading a 32 MB `evict_buffer` in between measurements.

> I'm aware on a theoretical level that iteration can be faster in scenarios of under ~100 entries, but I wouldn't expect members to be one of those use-cases. Either way, iterating `Member *` sounds like potentially tens of cache misses, which I'd expect to blow up costs under a real test. If anything, perhaps `Pair<StringName, Member*>` could work?

Well, the trick is to start at an index in the vector based on the already available hash. Here's the core idea:
```c++
uint32_t slot_idx = p_name.hash() & capacity_mask;
while (const Member *member = data[slot_idx]) {
	if (member->name == p_name) {
		return member;
	}
	slot_idx = (slot_idx + 1) & capacity_mask;
}
return nullptr;
```
It's an [open addressing table](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_addressing). That said, it could definitely be worth storing `Pair<StringName, Member*>` values in this `Vector`, because it would indeed make caching more effective again (but of course it uses more memory and slows down growing due to `StringName` reference counting).

> Yes, and I really hope i haven't come across as too dismissive!

Definitely not. I respect the boundaries you've put in place to keep this project manageable and going in the right direction, and even if I did trip on some rules you've been very to the point and helpful.

> Feel free to come by [`#core`](https://chat.godotengine.org/channel/core) too if you're interested in talking about it more.

Thanks, I've joined the channel, feel free to ping me there if it's more convenient than GitHub comments.  :-)
